<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-06w">
  <title>Week 6: Multiple integrals; vector calculus</title>

<subsection>
<title>Double Integrals and Signed Volume</title>

<p>
The definite integral of a function of one variable measures signed area.
For functions of two variables, a double integral measures signed volume.
</p>

<definition>
<p>

For a function <m>f(x,y)</m> defined on a region <m>R</m>,

<me>
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dA
</me>

represents the accumulated value of <m>f</m> over the region.

</p>
</definition>

<image width="50%">
<shortdescription>A three-dimensional sketch showing a planar region labeled R with x-, y-, and z-axes.</shortdescription>
<latex-image>
  \begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(-1.5,-1.5) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.2,0) node[left]{$y$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3) node[above]{$z$};
\draw[fill=gray!20] (1,-.5) ellipse (1 and .4);
\node at (1,-.5){$R$};
\draw[thick,fill=blue!20,fill opacity=.5] plot[smooth cycle] coordinates {(0.5,0.2) (0,0.7) (1,1) (2,1.5) (1.5,0.5) };
\node at (1,1.5){$S$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Iterated Integrals</title>

<p>
Most double integrals you will encounter are evaluated as repeated one-variable integrals.
</p>

<theorem>

<me>
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dA
=
\int_a^b\int_{c(x)}^{d(x)} f(x,y)\,dy\,dx.
</me>

</theorem>

<example>

<statement>

Evaluate

<me>
\int_0^1\int_0^2 (x+y)\,dy\,dx.
</me>

</statement>
<solution>
  <me>
\int_0^1\int_0^2 (x+y)\,dy\,dx = \int_0^1 \left[xy + \frac{y^2}{2}\right]_0^2 dx =
</me>
  
<me>
= \int_0^1 (2x + 2) dx = \left[x^2 + 2x\right]_0^1 = 3.
</me>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Changing the Order of Integration</title>

<p>
Sometimes reversing the order of integration greatly simplifies a problem.
</p>

<activity>

Sketch the region described by

<me>
\int_0^1
\int_x^1
f(x,y)\,dy\,dx.
</me>

Rewrite the integral with the order reversed.
<solution>
  <image width="50%">
    <shortdescription>Two triangular regions in the xy-plane showing the original and reversed order of integration for a double integral.</shortdescription>
    <latex-image>
      \begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (-.5,0)--(1.5,0);
\draw[->] (0,-.5)--(0,1.5);
\draw[thick,fill=blue!20] (0,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle;
\draw[->,thick,blue] (0,0.5)--(1,0.5);
\node at (1.5,-0.2){$x$};
\node at (-0.2,1.5){$y$};

\draw[->] (-2.5,-0.5)--(-2.5,1.5);
\draw[->] (-3,0)--(-1,0);
\draw[thick,fill=red!20] (-2.5,0)--(-2.5,1)--(-1.5,1)--cycle;
\draw[->,thick,red] (-2,0)--(-2,1.5);
\node at (-1,-.2){$x$};
\node at (-2.7,1.5){$y$};
      \end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
  </image>
  <p>
The region is a triangle with vertices at (0,0), (1,1), and (0,1). The integral can be rewritten as
<md>
 \int_0^1
\int_x^1
f(x,y)\,dy\,dx=\int_0^1
\int_0^y
f(x,y)\,dx\,dy.
</md>

  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Applications</title>

<definition>

Average value of a function:

<me>
f_{avg}
=
\frac{1}{A(R)}
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dA.
</me>

</definition>

<example>
<p>
  
Suppose <m>f(x,y)</m> represents temperature distribution over a metal plate.
Interpret

<me>
\frac{1}{A(R)}
\iint_Rf\,dA.
</me>

</p>
</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>
  <title>Coordinates Type</title>
  <p>
    In this section we will discuss different coordinate systems that are useful for evaluating integrals in multivariable calculus.
  </p>
  
<subsubsection>
<title>Polar Coordinates</title>

<p>
Circular regions are often easier to describe using polar coordinates.
</p>

<definition>

<me>
x=r\cos\theta,
\qquad
y=r\sin\theta.
</me>

</definition>

<theorem>

<me>
dA=r\,dr\,d\theta.
</me>

</theorem>



<image width="40%">
<shortdescription>A polar coordinate diagram with a radius r, angle theta, and the Cartesian axes shown for a point in the plane.</shortdescription>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}

\draw[->] (-2,0)--(2,0);
\draw[->] (0,-2)--(0,2);

\draw (0,0) circle (1.4);

\draw[thick,->] (0,0)--(1,1);

\draw (.5,0) arc (0:42:.5);

\node at (.8,.25){$\theta$};
\node at (0.7,1){$r$};
\node at (1.7,-.5){$x$};
\node at (-.5,1.7){$y$};
      \end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

<example>
<p>
Evaluate

<me>
\iint_R (x^2+y^2)\,dA
</me>

where <m>R</m> is the unit disk.

</p>
<solution>
  <p>
    In polar coordinates, <m>x = r\cos\theta</m> and <m>y = r\sin\theta</m>, so <m>x^2 + y^2 = r^2</m>. The integral becomes
    <me>
      \iint_R (x^2+y^2)\,dA = \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^1 r^2 \cdot r\,dr\,d\theta = \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^1 r^3\,dr\,d\theta.
    </me>
    Evaluating the inner integral,
    <me>
      \int_0^1 r^3\,dr = \left[\frac{r^4}{4}\right]_0^1 = \frac{1}{4}.
    </me>
    Therefore,
    <me>
      \int_0^{2\pi} \frac{1}{4}\,d\theta = \frac{1}{4} \cdot 2\pi = \frac{\pi}{2}.
    </me>
  </p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsubsection>
<subsubsection>  
  <title>Cylindrical Coordinates</title>
  
  <p>
    Cylindrical coordinates are useful for describing surfaces of revolution and other 3D shapes with circular symmetry.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <p>
      Cylindrical coordinates are defined by
      <me>
        x = r\cos\theta, \quad y = r\sin\theta, \quad z = z.
      </me>
    </p>
  </definition>
  <image>
    <shortdescription>Cylindrical coordinate system diagram.</shortdescription>
    <latex-image>
      \begin{tikzpicture}
        \draw[->] (-2,0)--(2,0) node[right]{$y$};
        \draw[->] (0,-2)--(0,2) node[above]{$z$};
        \draw[->] (0,0)--(-1.2,-1.2) node[left]{$x$};

        \draw (0,0) ellipse (1.4 and 0.7);

        \draw[thick,->] (0,0)--(1.1,-0.45);

        \draw (-.25,-0.25) arc (-120:-40:.5);

        \node at (0.35,-.5){$\theta$};
        \node at (.75,-.15){$r$};
      \end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
  </image>
  <theorem>
    <p>
      The volume element in cylindrical coordinates is given by
      <me>
        dV = r\,dr\,d\theta\,dz.
      </me>
    </p>
  </theorem>
</subsubsection>
<subsubsection>
  <title>Spherical Coordinates</title>
  
  <p>
    Spherical coordinates are useful for describing spheres and other 3D shapes with radial symmetry.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <p>
      Spherical coordinates are defined by
      <me>
        x = \rho\sin\phi\cos\theta, \quad y = \rho\sin\phi\sin\theta, \quad z = \rho\cos\phi,
      </me>
      where <m>\rho</m> is the radial distance, <m>\phi</m> is the polar angle, and <m>\theta</m> is the azimuthal angle.
    </p>
  </definition>
  <image>
    <shortdescription>Spherical coordinate system diagram.</shortdescription>
    <latex-image>
    \begin{tikzpicture}
        \draw[->] (0,0)--(2,0) node[right]{$y$};
        \draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2) node[above]{$z$};
        \draw[->] (0,0)--(-1.2,-1.2) node[left]{$x$};
\draw[gray,dashed] (1,.9)--(1,-0.4);
\draw[gray,dashed] (0,0)--(1,-0.4);
        \draw (0,0) circle (1.5);
\draw[gray,dashed] (0,0) ellipse (1.5 and 0.7);
        \draw[thick,->] (0,0)--(1,.9);
\draw[fill] (1,0.9) circle (0.05);
        \draw (.35,0.34) arc (45:90:.5);
        \draw (-.25,-0.24) arc (-115:-40:.45);

        \node at (.2,.7){$\phi$};
        \node at (0,-.5){$\theta$};
        \node at (.55,.75){$\rho$};
      \end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
  </image>
  <theorem>
    <p>
      The volume element in spherical coordinates is given by
      <me>
        dV = \rho^2 \sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta.
      </me>
    </p>
  </theorem>
</subsubsection>

  </subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Vector Fields</title>

<p>
A vector field assigns a vector to every point in a region.
</p>

<definition>
<p>
<me>
\mathbf F(x,y)
=
\langle P(x,y),Q(x,y)\rangle.
</me>
is a vector-function of two variables with two components.</p>
</definition>

<example>
<p>
Interpret and graph the vector field

<me>
\mathbf F(x,y)=\langle -y,x\rangle.
</me>
</p>
<solution>
  <p>
    The vector field <m>\mathbf F(x,y)=\langle -y,x\rangle</m> represents a rotational flow around the origin. At any point <m>(x,y)</m>, the vector points in the direction of the tangent to the circle centered at the origin passing through that point.
  </p>
  <image>
  <shortdescription>Vector field diagram showing rotational arrows swirling around the origin in a clockwise pattern.</shortdescription>
  <latex-image>
      \begin{tikzpicture}
        \draw[->,thick] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right]{$x$};
        \draw[->,thick] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above]{$y$};
        \foreach \x in {-2,-1,0,1,2} {
          \foreach \y in {-2,-1,0,1,2} {
            \draw[->,blue] (\x,\y) -- ++(-\y,\x);
          }
        } 
      \end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
    
  </image>
</solution>
</example>


</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>The Gradient</title>

<p>
The gradient links scalar functions and vector fields.
</p>

<me>
\nabla f
=
\left\langle
f_x,
f_y
\right\rangle.
</me>

<theorem>

<p>The gradient points in the direction of greatest increase.
</p>
</theorem>

<example>
<p>
Find the gradient of

<me>
f(x,y)=x^2+y^2.
</me>
</p>
<solution>
  <p>
    The gradient is computed as follows:
    <me>
      \nabla f = \left\langle \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \right\rangle = \left\langle 2x, 2y \right\rangle.
    </me>
    Therefore, the gradient vector at any point <m>(x,y)</m> is <m>\langle 2x, 2y \rangle</m>, which points radially outward from the origin, indicating the direction of greatest increase of the function <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2</m>.
  </p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Divergence</title>

<p>
Divergence measures expansion or compression of a vector field.
</p>

<definition>
<p>Divergence is defined as
<me>
Div(\mathbf F) = \nabla\cdot \mathbf F
=
\frac{\partial P}{\partial x}
+
\frac{\partial Q}{\partial y}.
</me>
</p>
</definition>


<sidebyside>



<image width="30%">
<shortdescription>Positive divergence:
vectors spread outward.</shortdescription>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(1,0);
\draw[->] (0,0.5)--(1,1);
\draw[->] (0,1)--(1,1.2);
\draw[->] (0,-0.5)--(1,-1);
\draw[->] (0,-1)--(1,-1.2);
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>



<image width="30%">
<shortdescription>Negative divergence:
vectors move inward.</shortdescription>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(1,0);
\draw[->] (0,-.5)--(1,-0.2);
\draw[->] (0,-1)--(1,-0.7);
\draw[->] (0,.5)--(1,0.2);
\draw[->] (0,1)--(1,0.7);

\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>


</sidebyside>


<example>
<p>
Compute the divergence of

<me>
\mathbf F
=
\langle x,y\rangle.
</me>
</p>
<solution>
  <p>
    The divergence is computed as follows:
    <me>
      Div(\mathbf F) = \nabla\cdot \mathbf F = \frac{\partial x}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial y}{\partial y} = 1 + 1 = 2.
    </me>
    Therefore, the divergence of the vector field <m>\mathbf F = \langle x, y \rangle</m> is constant and equal to 2, indicating that the field is uniformly expanding at every point in the plane.
  </p>
</solution> 

</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Curl</title>

<p>
Curl measures local rotation.
</p>

<definition>
<p>
For a vector field <m> \mathbf F = \langle P(x,y), Q(x,y), R(x,y) \rangle</m>, the curl is defined as

<me>
\operatorname{curl}(\mathbf F)=\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i &amp; \mathbf j &amp; \mathbf k \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} &amp; \frac{\partial}{\partial y} &amp; \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
P &amp; Q &amp; R
\end{vmatrix}
</me><me>
= 
\left\langle
\frac{\partial R}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z},
\frac{\partial P}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial R}{\partial x},
\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y}
\right\rangle.
</me>
</p>
</definition>

<image width="45%">
<shortdescription>A closed loop of arrows illustrating the local rotation of a vector field around a point.</shortdescription>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}

\draw[->] (0,0)--(1,.5);
\draw[->] (1,.5)--(.5,1.5);
\draw[->] (.5,1.5)--(-.5,1);
\draw[->] (-.5,1)--(0,0);

\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>


<example>
<p>
Find the curl of

<me>
\mathbf F
=
\langle -y,x,x+yz\rangle.
</me>
</p>
<solution>
  <p>
    The curl is computed as follows:
    <me>
      \operatorname{curl}(\mathbf F) = \nabla \times \mathbf F = \left\langle \frac{\partial (x+yz)}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial x}{\partial z}, \frac{\partial (-y)}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial (x+yz)}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial
  x}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial (-y)}{\partial x} \right\rangle = \left\langle z - 0, 0 - 1, 0 - 0 \right\rangle = \langle z, -1, 0 \rangle.
    </me>
  </p>
</solution>

</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Line Integrals</title>

<p>
A line integral adds values along a curve.
</p>

<definition>

<me>
\int_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r.
</me>

</definition>

<p>
Physical interpretation:
work done by a force field.
</p>

<example>
<p>
Compute the work done by

<me>
\mathbf F=\langle 1,2\rangle
</me>

along a straight path.
</p>
<solution>
  <p>
    The work done by the constant force field <m>\mathbf F = \langle 1, 2 \rangle</m> along a straight path from point <m>A</m> to point <m>B</m> can be computed using the line integral:
    <me>
      W = \int_C \mathbf F \cdot d\mathbf r = \mathbf F \cdot \mathbf d = \langle 1, 2 \rangle \cdot \langle x_B - x_A, y_B - y_A \rangle = 1(x_B - x_A) + 2(y_B - y_A).
    </me>
    This formula gives the total work done by the force field along the straight path from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m>.
  </p>
</solution>

</example>


</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Conservative Vector Fields</title>

<definition>
<p>

A vector field is conservative if

<me>
\mathbf F=\nabla f
</me>

for some potential function <m>f</m>. 
</p>

</definition>

<theorem>
  <p>
For a conservative field,

<me>
\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
=
f(B)-f(A).
</me>
</p>

</theorem>

<activity>
<p>

Find a potential function for

<me>
\mathbf F=\langle 2x,2y\rangle.
</me>
</p>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Big Picture</title>


<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>A flow diagram showing scalar field f leading to the gradient, divergence, and curl of a vector field.</shortdescription>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=3cm]

\node (f) {$f$};

\node[right of=f] (g) {$\nabla f$};

\node[right of=g] (d) {$\nabla\cdot \mathbf F$};

\node[below of=g] (c) {$\mathrm{curl}(\mathbf F)$};

\draw[->] (f)--(g);
\draw[->] (g)--(d);
\draw[->] (g)--(c);

\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="integral-theorems">
  <title>Integral Theorems in Multivariable Calculus</title>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>Overview</title>

    <p>
      Integral theorems connect local derivative information
      (gradient, curl, divergence) with global quantities such as
      line, surface, and volume integrals. They provide powerful
      tools for converting difficult integrals into easier ones.
    </p>

      <image width="60%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \draw (0,0) rectangle (10,6);
          \node at (5,3) {Derivative};

          \draw (15,0) rectangle (25,6);
          \node at (20,3) {Integral};
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
      </image>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>The Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals</title>

    <statement>
      If <m>\mathbf{F} = \nabla f</m> is a gradient field and
      <m>C</m> is a curve from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m>, then
      <me>
        \int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = f(B)-f(A).
      </me>
    </statement>

    <p>
      The line integral depends only on the endpoints, not on the path.
    </p>

      <image width="45%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
        % Endpoints 
        \fill (0,0) circle (2pt); 
        \node[below left] at (0,0) {$A$}; 
        \fill (6,2) circle (2pt); 
        \node[above right] at (6,2) {$B$}; 
        % Path 
        \draw[blue, thick, ->] (0,0) .. controls (2,-1) and (4,3) .. (6,2); 
        \node[blue] at (3,1.8) {$C$}; 
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
      </image>

  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>Green's Theorem</title>

    <statement>
      For a positively oriented simple closed curve <m>C</m>
      enclosing a region <m>R</m>,
      <me>
        \oint_C (P\,dx + Q\,dy)
        =
        \iint_R
        \left(
        \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}
        -
        \frac{\partial P}{\partial y}
        \right)\,dA.
      </me>
    </statement>

    <p>
      Green's Theorem converts a boundary line integral into a
      double integral over the enclosed region.
    </p>

      <image width="50%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
% Region 
\fill[yellow!20] (0,0) ellipse (3cm and 2cm); 
\draw (0,0) ellipse (3cm and 2cm); 
\node at (0,0) {$R$}; 
% Boundary with orientation arrow 
\draw[red,very thick,->] (3,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=320, x radius=3cm,y radius=2cm]; 
\node[red] at (3.5,0) {$C$}; 
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
      </image>
   
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>Stokes' Theorem</title>

    <statement>
      Let <m>S</m> be an oriented surface with boundary
      <m>\partial S</m>. Then
      <me>
        \oint_{\partial S}
        \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
        =
        \iint_S
        (\nabla\times\mathbf{F})
        \cdot \mathbf{n}\,dS.
      </me>
    </statement>

    <p>
      Stokes' Theorem generalizes Green's Theorem to surfaces in
      three dimensions.
    </p>

      <image width="55%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
% Surface 
\fill[blue!20] (0,0) ellipse (3cm and 1.3cm); 
\fill[blue!20] (0,0.5) ellipse (2.7cm and 1.3cm); 
\draw[red, dashed] (0,0) ellipse (3cm and 1.3cm); 
% Boundary 
\draw[->,red,very thick] (-3,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=357,x radius=2.98cm,y radius=1.3cm]; 
\node[red] at (3.4,-0.2) {$\partial S$}; 
\node at (0,-0.2) {$S$}; 
% Normal vector 
\draw[thick,->] (0,1.7) -- (0,2.2); 
\node[right] at (0,2) {$\vec{\mathbf n}$}; 
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
      </image>

  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>Divergence Theorem</title>

    <statement>
      If <m>E</m> is a solid region with boundary surface
      <m>\partial E</m>, then
      <me>
        \iint_{\partial E}
        \mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{n}\,dS
        =
        \iiint_E
        (\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F})\,dV.
      </me>
    </statement>

    <p>
      The total outward flux through a closed surface equals the
      accumulated divergence inside the volume.
    </p>

      <image width="50%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
% Region E 
\draw (0,0) ellipse (2.8cm and 3.2cm); 
% Outward-pointing flux vectors 
\foreach \t in {0,30,...,330} { \draw[blue,thick,->] ({2.8*cos(\t)},{3.2*sin(\t)}) -- ({3.6*cos(\t)},{4.1*sin(\t)}); } 
% Label 
\node at (0,0) {$E$}; 
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
      </image>
 
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>Summary</title>

    <ul>
      <li>
        Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals:
        gradient ↔ line integral.
      </li>
      <li>
        Green's Theorem:
        boundary curve ↔ planar region.
      </li>
      <li>
        Stokes' Theorem:
        boundary curve ↔ surface.
      </li>
      <li>
        Divergence Theorem:
        closed surface ↔ volume.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <md>
      Gradient → Line Integral → Green/Stokes → Divergence
    </md>
  </subsubsection>
</subsection>



</section>
